I'm tracking down a bug in the USB Type-C partner registration logic where `typec_unregister_partner` unconditionally unlinks the partner even when the link was never actually created. The issue stems from a timing mismatch: the link only gets established during `typec_register_partner` if the USB device is already attached, or later during `typec_partner_attach`, but the unregister path doesn't check whether a link was ever made before trying to remove it. The scenario involves `typec_partner_attach` setting the USB device pointer, which then triggers the unlink attempt.

The real problem emerges when the USB device gets removed without `typec_partner_deattach` being called to clean up the stale pointer—the sysfs directory for the USB device is already gone, so the unlink fails with a NULL parent kobj. I need to check how the port-mapper handles device removal to understand why deattach isn't being invoked in all cases.
